<ARM_IP_install_area> is where you choose to install ARM IP. It is 
recommended to install all ARM simulation models into this same
directory.

Contents in simulation_models/DSM/mti_modelsim_verilog_SunOS/ARM7TDMI_unlicensed_3A-00
(this directory is pointed by the environment variable $ARM7TDMI_HOME) :


   README       - this file

   ARM7TDMI  	- contains all required model files
   		     See paragraph "About simulator wrappers" for details
   
   testing 	- contains crftester model files, one crf vector file 
   		     and its associated ctrm file
   
   docs		- contains device related README_2, CRF readme file,
   		     DSM User Guide and Model Manager application notes.
   
   SDF_TOOLS    - contains backannotation tools sdfgen and sdfremap

   setup.(c)sh  - setup files





===================================================================
             Setting up The Model
===================================================================

1) Set the environment variable ARM7TDMI_HOME to point to the release directory.

   The release directory is the directory where the current README file is, 
   and where the directory structure mentioned in the previous paragraph 
   is available. 

   Go into the release directory : 
                cd <release directory>

   Set the environment variable : 

         C   shell :     setenv ARM7TDMI_HOME `pwd`
      Bourne shell :     ARM7TDMI_HOME=`pwd` 
                         export ARM7TDMI_HOME 

2) Setup the environment, by sourcing the setup script

         C   shell :     source $ARM7TDMI_HOME/setup.csh
      Bourne shell :        .   $ARM7TDMI_HOME/setup.sh
 
   Edit the modelsim.ini file to include the lines : 
            [vsim]                                              
            Veriuser = $MG_LIB/mti_modelsim_verilog/libmgmm.so
 
   Note that the environment variable DIR_ARM7TDMI is created and points at 
   "release_directory"/ARM7TDMI, the directory where all the model files are 
   included.





===================================================================
    Testing The Model Setup - CrfTester
===================================================================

The supplied crftester can be used to test the model.

1) Setup the simulator environment.


2) Run the crftester script 
      - SDF timing : 
             $ARM7TDMI_HOME/testing/crftest.csh
      - NO timing : 
             $ARM7TDMI_HOME/testing/crftest_extra.csh


3) The crftester simulation should run the vector set with
   '0 vectors failed', and the following completion message should be 
   displayed : 
        
          "The ARM7TDMI DSM is correctly setup"

  An example of crftester simulation log trace is supplied :
      - SDF timing : 
          $ARM7TDMI_HOME/testing/reference_crftest.log	
      - No timing : 
          $ARM7TDMI_HOME/testing/reference_crftest_extra.log



===================================================================
            Use The model
===================================================================

1) Setup the simulator environment.

2) Compile the HDL files.
   Two separate HDL wrappers are supplied. Select the appropriate 
   one when compiling/elaborating your design. 

      - HDL wrapper with SDF timing shell (also called SDF timing wrapper):
          ARM7TDMI/ARM7TDMI.v    
      - HDL wrapper without timing shell (also called no-timing wrapper):   
          ARM7TDMI/extra_wrapper/ARM7TDMI.v  

   Note that the SDF timing file template is available in the 
   directory "ARM7TDMI". ARM7TDMI.tdef file only contains example timing values 
   and are not representative of any given process or characterization.

3) The crftester simulation run when testing the setup of the DSM shows 
   an example of how to compile/elaborate and run the DSM :
      - SDF timing : 
            $ARM7TDMI_HOME/testing/crftest.csh
      - No timing : 
            $ARM7TDMI_HOME/testing/crftest_extra.csh


===================================================================
         Documentation
===================================================================

The following documentation is supplied with the Model in  
the directory $ARM7TDMI_HOME/docs : 
       - device related documentation in the 'README_2' file (if supplied)
       - ModelManager documentation in 'MM_mti_modelsim_verilog.txt' 
       - CRF vectors and the associated '.ctrm' file in 'crf_file.readme' 
       - sdfgen and sdfremap as manpages
       - DSM User Guide

Note that $ARM7TDMI_HOME is the DSM installation directory.

NOTE:
Documentation about setting up the appropriate timing model can be found 
also in the ModelManager documentation in 'docs/MM_mti_modelsim_verilog.txt' 
(section labelled : Timing)


===================================================================
     About simulator wrappers - Withdrawal of the TDEF timing
===================================================================

The simulator wrapper HDL files in this model are arranged differently
to previous models. 

Previous models contained two wrapper files, one called
"ARM7TDMI_sdf.v" for timing annotation from SDF, and one called 
"ARM7TDMI.v" for annotation from a TDEF file using the built-in
timing shell.  For earlier releases, these were both located in the
"ARM7TDMI" directory, and for more recent releases the SDF wrapper was
renamed to "ARM7TDMI.v" and located in the "ARM7TDMI" directory 
while the built-in timing wrapper in the "ARM7TDMI/extra" directory.

From this release, TDEF annotation has been withdrawn and instead a
simulator wrapper which contains no timing shell is provided in
"ARM7TDMI/extra_wrapper" directory.  This file can be used to run the 
model with no timing annotated.

===================================================================
               Important Points
===================================================================

1)  This DSM does not support restart or checkpointing.
    
2)  DSMs are only intended to be used for functional and timing simulation,
    they are not designed for fault simulation or static timing analysis.
    
3)  It is imperative that you don't mix parts of the DSM with earlier 
    versions, this will lead to the DSM not working correctly!!  Only use
    the HDL wrapper file ARM7TDMI.v  that was supplied 
    with the current version of the DSM, with supplied version of 
    library (for example ARM7TDMI.so) and the supplied version
    of the Model Manager.
    
4)  If you have more than one DSM in a simulation and one of the DSMs is 
    older than the other, always make sure that you are using the Model
    Manager from the most recent version, otherwise the Model Manager
    will not load the most recent shared object library.
    
5)  When using LD_LIBRARY_PATH (or SHLIB_PATH) environment variable and 
    this variable is already set up, it is important to make sure that
    the path of the Model Manager appears as the first item in the path.
    
6)  For Modelsim VHDL DSM, if you want to run with the 64-bit version 
    of the simulator, make sure that you have the 64-bit version of the 
    DSM.  This will be indicated in the version information at the top
    of the README file, eg, 
    
    	 Platform :  SunOS-64
    
    If this just says SunOS, then this model is only intended to be used
    with the 32-bit version of the simulator.  The 32-bit version of the 
    DSM will not run under the 64-bit version of vsim.
    
7)  For Modelsim VHDL DSM, when compiling with VHDL/SDF wrapper, make
    sure you use
    	vcom -novitalcheck
    otherwise the wrapper won't compile correctly.
    
8)  The DSM ARM produced should run on Solaris 2.5.1, 2.6, 7 or 8 in 32-bit
    mode.  We don't support 64-bit mode except for Modelsim VHDL on 
    Solaris 8.  It's also worth noting that not all simulators support all 
    of these Solaris versions.  For example. newer Synopsys simulators
    don't support 2.5.1 or 2.6,  and newer Cadence simulators don't support
    2.5.1.
    
9)  Do not modify the HDL wrapper (eg ARM7TDMI.v) at all.  Doing so might
    result in the model failing in a subtle way.  We cannot support the DSM 
    when the wrappers have been modified. Please ref to 
       $ARM7TDMI_HOME/docs/README_2 
    regarding how to modify the model's generics/defparam.
    
10) Do not try and enable the Built-In Timing shell when using the HDL/SDF
    wrapper.  In other words, do not set the MG_ENABLE_BUILTIN_TIMING = 1
    within the HDL/SDF wrapper file, otherwise the DSM will not work 
    correctly.  Always make sure this parameter/generics is set to 0 when
    using this type of wrapper.


===================================================================
          Common Warnings and Errors
===================================================================

1)  When Installing the Models
    
    > ERROR: ARM7TDMI_sdf.vhd(1053): VITAL timing generic size does not match
    > product of associated port sizes ( 32 /= 32 * 32 ) 
    > (1076.4 section 4.3.2.1.2)
    > ERROR: ARM7TDMI_sdf.vhd(1248): VHDL Compiler exiting 
    
    This error is produced when compiling with vcom using the Modelsim HDL/SDF
    wrapper file without the -novitalcheck switch. To correct this:
    
     vcom -novitalcheck ARM7TDMI.vhd

2)  When Simulating the Models 

    When simulating Swift/VhMC/VMC based DSMs there will be lots of warning 
    messages at time 0 when the model starts up. In general all warning 
    messages at time 0 until the model has been properly reset can be ignored. 

    For example :
    
    > Warning! 0 fs - ARM7TDMICRFTESTERTOP.theARM7TDMI.thetiming.theinout:
    > 
    > Assertion WARNING at 0 PS in design unit XXXX from process XXXX: 
    > [MG_Model-MGWARNING] "/arm/chips/dsm/gwang/work/ARM7TDMIr3_vhmc/
    > 	ModelGen/collated/r3/ve...
    
    These type of warning messages arise from the Swift/VhMC/VMC component 
    within the DSM which hasn't initialized properly.

    
    > " To_Integer --- 'X' encountered in the input vector. Returning zero result."
    > " To_Integer --- 'X' encountered in the input vector. Returning zero result."

    At time 0 those can also be safely ignored. These type of warning messages 
    arise from the Swift/VhMC/VMC component within the DSM when the model is 
    initializing at time 0.

    > # Output violation on nEXEC
    > # ModelGen Warning, Time 74376852 ps, Instance

    Output violation messages are only associated with the Built-In timing 
    shell, they won't be produced when using the HDL/SDF timing shell.
    These messages occur when the timing shell for the model fails
    to match an output transition with an input causal event and therefore
    can't assign an appropriate time to the output. This could arise 
    due to two possible reasons, these are:

      i) The model hasn't been reset properly. Or
      ii) A timing path is missing in the timing shell for the model.
    
      In this situation the  Built-In timing shell can't find a delay 
      value to annotate and the default value is used for the output. In 
      practice this means the output has zero delay.

    For ARM7TDMI DSM, it is important to properly reset the core at the 
    start of the simulation.  This is done by pulsing nTRST signal low 
    and then high as well as the nRESET signal. 
